    {
      deepDive:"This unit connects formal language, number representation and theories of knowing. Place value is a mathematical representation: a symbol's contribution changes with its position. Zero can mark an empty position and can also be used as a number. Historical number-encoding systems use conventions that must be learned before decoding. Panini's grammar demonstrates a highly organized rule system; comparing it with computation can reveal how rules generate forms, but the comparison does not make the ancient work a modern programming language. Pingala's patterns of short and long syllables are similarly connected to combinatorics through a clear analogy. Context protects the historical meaning while allowing students to notice structural similarities.",
      examples:[
        {title:"Explain why zero matters in 205",prompt:"Show how zero changes the place-value reading.",steps:["Expand 205 by place: 2×100 + 0×10 + 5×1.","The zero says there are no tens but keeps the tens position present.","Without the placeholder, the written form 25 would mean 2 tens and 5 ones.","Thus 205 and 25 represent different quantities."],answer:"Zero serves as a placeholder and also has numeric value in arithmetic."},
        {title:"Decode a conventional number code",prompt:"Your class gives a word-to-digit mapping. How should you explain a decoded example?",steps:["State the specific mapping provided in the lesson or question.","Identify which word or symbol maps to each digit.","Apply the stated order to form the number; do not assume a universal mapping.","Check that the decoded number follows the convention's direction."],answer:"The method depends on the convention. A reliable answer states the mapping before using it."}
      ],
      diagrams:[
        {type:"grid",title:"Place value keeps empty positions",caption:"The zero holds the tens place so 205 does not collapse into 25.",columns:["Hundreds","Tens","Ones"],rows:[["Digit","2","0","5"],["Contribution","200","0","5"]]},
        {type:"flow",title:"A knowledge process",caption:"The terms describe what is to be known, a means of knowing it and uncertainty that may need resolution.",labels:["Prameya · object","Samshaya · doubt","Pramana · means","Knowledge claim"]}
      ]
    },
    {
      deepDive:"Historical mathematics and astronomy can be studied through the questions people were trying to answer. Construction requires measuring and transforming shapes; calendars require organizing observations and time; observatories require instruments that connect geometry with sight lines. The course brings these together through the Sulba Sutras, named mathematicians and works, Panchanga terminology and Jantar Mantar. The best exam answers are specific: name a text or instrument, describe the method or measure, and situate it in its historical purpose. Do not claim a modern theorem or precision unless the source demonstrates that claim. A five-part Panchanga answer is especially suited to a structured response because each limb contributes a distinct calendar measure.",
      examples:[
        {title:"Explain all five Panchanga limbs",prompt:"Write a complete five-part answer.",steps:["Tithi: a lunar day; vara: the weekday.","Nakshatra: a lunar mansion; yoga: a calculated angular relationship.","Karana: half of a tithi.","Conclude that the five measures combine to organize calendrical information."],answer:"A Panchanga is a framework combining these five calendar limbs; it is more than a simple date list."},
        {title:"Describe a Sulba Sutra geometry example",prompt:"Prepare a historically careful explanation.",steps:["Name the Sulba Sutras and their altar-construction setting.","Describe the specific measurement or shape procedure provided by your course source.","Explain the practical geometric idea involved, such as measuring or transforming a shape.","Do not call a rule a modern proof unless the source supports that interpretation."],answer:"Context, procedure and evidence make the historical explanation precise."}
      ],
      diagrams:[
        {type:"tree",title:"Panchanga's five calendar clues",caption:"Each limb contributes a different measure; together they organize a traditional calendar.",root:"Panchanga",branches:["Tithi · lunar day","Vara · weekday","Nakshatra · lunar mansion","Yoga · angular relation","Karana · half tithi"]},
        {type:"flow",title:"From question to instrument reading",caption:"An astronomical instrument links a designed structure to observation and calculation.",labels:["Question about time / sky","Instrument geometry","Observe position","Calculate / record","Use in calendar"]}
      ]
    },
    {
      deepDive:"Material and architectural knowledge is best explained as a process with a purpose. A metal answer can follow material selection, preparation, heating or smelting, shaping, joining and finishing. A dye or painting answer can describe the color material, surface and application technique. Built spaces add site, circulation, structure, materials and symbolism. These processes varied by period, region and craft tradition, so use a specific example from class instead of making a universal claim. Vastu Shastra should be discussed as historical architectural texts and principles; modern engineering performance requires modern evidence. This careful method shows both respect for historical knowledge and accuracy about what the source establishes.",
      examples:[
        {title:"Write a process-based metallurgy answer",prompt:"Explain an example of iron or steel work without overclaiming.",steps:["Name the material and a documented use from class.","Describe the supported process: ore or material preparation, heating/smelting, shaping and finishing.","Connect the technique to tools, artisans, trade or social needs.","State the time or region when known and avoid claiming universal superiority."],answer:"A process-plus-context answer is more informative than a list of metals."},
        {title:"Describe a temple plan",prompt:"Explain how common elements relate in one tradition.",steps:["Name the style or region if your source provides it.","Identify elements such as entrance, hall, sanctum and tower/superstructure.","Describe how movement or layout links the elements.","Mention that forms vary and explain material or craft choices from your example."],answer:"Use the elements as parts of one design; avoid implying every temple has an identical plan."}
      ],
      diagrams:[
        {type:"flow",title:"A material becomes a crafted object",caption:"Different traditions use different techniques; the sequence shows the kinds of decisions an answer can explain.",labels:["Choose material","Prepare / heat","Shape / join","Finish surface","Use / trade"]},
        {type:"tree",title:"Built-space elements",caption:"A site plan combines structural, functional and symbolic features; the exact form varies by region.",root:"Temple / built space",branches:["Site + orientation","Entry + halls","Sanctum","Tower / superstructure","Craft + sculpture"]}
      ]
    },
    {
      deepDive:"This unit connects personal wellbeing with ideas about psychology, ethics and public administration. Keep each framework in its own context. Ayurveda and yoga traditions discuss daily practices such as food, water and rest; the course may describe these as historical or philosophical approaches, not personal medical instructions. Triguna offers a vocabulary for qualities of mind and nature. The Ramayana and Arthashastra offer different textual perspectives on duty and governance. A strong comparative answer first describes a text's own concerns and historical setting, then identifies a point of comparison with modern institutions. This prevents an anachronism: a classical prescription and a current constitution answer questions in different social and political contexts.",
      examples:[
        {title:"Apply the Triguna terms to a short illustration",prompt:"A person feels calm and clear, then later becomes restless and driven. Use the framework carefully.",steps:["Use sattva for the state associated with clarity and balance.","Use rajas for activity and desire; explain the change in state.","If discussing heaviness or inertia, use tamas with its course definition.","Say these are traditional conceptual qualities, not a clinical diagnosis."],answer:"The example demonstrates vocabulary while preserving the framework's limits."},
        {title:"Compare classical and modern governance",prompt:"Use Arthashastra as a historical source and compare one administrative question.",steps:["Name the Arthashastra and its association with statecraft.","Describe a specific topic such as officials, revenue, law or diplomacy.","Name the modern question being compared, such as public revenue or administration.","Explain one similarity in the problem and one difference in institutions or context."],answer:"A careful comparison does not treat the text as a current constitutional rulebook."}
      ],
      diagrams:[
        {type:"compare",title:"Triguna as a conceptual framework",caption:"These are traditional descriptions of qualities, not scores on a modern diagnostic scale.",leftTitle:"Sattva",leftItems:["Clarity","Balance","Calm attention"],rightTitle:"Rajas / Tamas",rightItems:["Rajas · activity, desire","Tamas · inertia, obscurity","Context matters"]},
        {type:"compare",title:"Historical text and current institution",caption:"Compare a question or theme while preserving the different historical setting.",leftTitle:"Classical text",leftItems:["Historical setting","Normative ideas","Its own institutions"],rightTitle:"Modern system",rightItems:["Current legal context","Constitutional rules","Different institutions"]}
      ]
    }
